To combat the smuggling of cigarettes and other goods across the Cambodian-Vietnamese border, the General Department of Customs and Excise of Cambodia signed a memorandum of understanding in April 2011 with Vietnam, promising to Intensify border controls.
The World Health Organization is also lobbying the Cambodian government to curb cigarette smuggling.
The organization is trying to persuade the government to raise cigarette taxes to keep people from smoking.
The reason why smuggling into Vietnam from Cambodia is so lucrative is because smugglers can take advantage of tax differences by raising cigarette prices to a level that can be resold.
In the past, the Cambodian government has said it could not raise taxes on cigarettes because it would cause farmers to lose their jobs.
Cigarette sellers in Cambodia have to pay three types of taxes to the Customs and Excise Department, based on an assessment of the brand of cigarettes made by the government.
Those taxes include a 7 percent import tax, a 10 percent special import tax and a 10 percent value added tax.
Under the current tax system, Cambodia imposes the lowest tax on tobacco in the world at 10 percent of the retail price of cigarettes, he said.
That compares with Vietnam at 32.6 percent, Turkey at 65 percent and the United Kingdom at 81 percent.
Due to the decline in the amount of cigarettes legally imported into Cambodia in recent years, the General Department of Customs and Excise collected 12.78% less than the revenue in 2011 of only $ 40.65 million. Compared to two years ago, revenue rose to $ 46.61 million.
