Preliminary estimate data for the 2012-2013 Industrial Crops Compilation, prepared by the Department of Planning and Statistics of the Ministry of Agriculture, showed that the new season cassava cultivation was 346,594 hectares nationwide, a decrease. From the previous season 2011-2012, there were up to 391,714 hectares.
According to a report by the Ministry of Agriculture, the province with the highest cassava cultivation is Kampong Cham with an area of 67,427 hectares, Battambang with 57,020 hectares and Banteay Meanchey with 44,473 hectares.
Khem Chenda, director of the Ministry of Agriculture's administrative department, said the decline was due to lower cassava prices, which had led farmers to switch to other crops.
However, he claimed that prices could improve this year.
Sarun Chanthou, a cassava trader in Banteay Meanchey province's Svay Chek district, said that in general, cassava in the province generally seemed poorer than last year because of the floods at the end of 2012.
He said the price of dried cassava was between 625 riel and 650 riel per kilogram, while wet cassava was about 230 riel per kilogram, which is similar to 2012.
In January 2012, the Thai government required Thai traders to buy cassava from the people at 360 riel per kilogram, which affected prices in Cambodia.
Cassava prices soared in 2014 to 450 riel per kilogram for wet cassava amid sinking millions of tonnes of orders from China and the establishment of a processing plant in Cambodia.
The news has led most Cambodian farmers to give up other crops to grow cassava, but prices have fallen since 2012 due to overcrowding, while the Cambodian market is almost entirely dependent. Based on Thailand and Vietnam.
