Bou Sopheap, a coffee farmer in Mondulkiri province who owns two hectares of coffee plantations, said he had just finished harvesting the harvest, which yielded four tonnes in 2012, an increase of one tonne.
Chay Mao, head of a coffee processing handicraft and owner of more than five hectares of coffee plantations in the province, said he had just finished harvesting coffee a few days ago without weighing the harvest. He estimates that output is higher than last year.
He said that there are many factors that make the coffee crop more productive this year: attention, including pruning, water maintenance, fertilization, and lastly, good weather.
This handicraft produces about 900 kg to 1 ton of black coffee per month.
This is an increase from last year, which was only 700 to 800 kilograms per month.
He said Mondulkiri-produced coffee was gaining popularity in the market, so it encouraged farmers to take care of the remaining coffee from destruction to grow other crops.
